Pecuniary damages are those  which the victim has actually incurred and which are  capable of being calculated in terms of money;
 whereas non-pecuniary damages are those which are Raju (Dar) vs Ranjit Singh (158/18, S.L.C.) on 13 May, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/83404187/
7  incapable of being assessed by arithmetical  calculations.

This means that the Tribunal MACT No. 846/2018 Raju Vs. Ranjeet & Ors.. Page No. 15 of 41  should consider and decide with reference to the  evidence:
 (i) whether the disablement is permanent or  temporary;
 (ii) if the disablement is permanent, whether it is  permanent total disablement or permanent partial  disablement;
 (iii) if the disablement percentage is expressed with Raju (Dar) vs Ranjit Singh (158/18, S.L.C.) on 13 May, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/83404187/
11  reference to any specific limb, then the effect of  such disablement of the limb on the functioning of  the entire body, that is, the permanent disability  suffered by the person.
 If the Tribunal concludes that there is no  permanent disability then there is no question of  proceeding further and determining the loss of  future earning capacity.
